INDIC(3)                   indic.h manual                   INDIC(3)

NAME
    indic.h - colors, progress bars and spinners for the terminal

SYNOPSIS
    #include "indic.h"

    int  indic_init(void);
    void indic_progress(double frac, int width);
    void indic_spinner_tick(const char *msg);
    void indic_spinner_done(const char *msg);
    void indic_cursor_hide(void);
    void indic_cursor_show(void);

DESCRIPTION
    One header file. No dependencies beyond libc and the OS headers.
    Works on Linux, macOS and Windows (enables ANSI on the console).

    Call indic_init() once at the top of main(). Colors turn off by
    themselves when output is piped or NO_COLOR is set. The cursor and
    colors are restored on exit and on Ctrl+C.

    INDIC_PAINT(INDIC_RED, "text") wraps text and always resets.
    Colors: RED GREEN YELLOW BLUE MAGENTA CYAN WHITE BOLD DIM.
    These are string literals and always emit escapes; use
    indic_style(INDIC_RED) with %s if output may be piped.

    indic_progress(0.5, 30) redraws one line, newline at 1.0.
    A width of 0 fits the terminal.

    Define INDIC_ASCII before including for '#' and '-' bars.
    Include indic.h before other system headers.

EXAMPLE
    indic_init();
    printf(INDIC_PAINT(INDIC_GREEN, "ok\n"));
    for (i = 0; i <= 100; i++) indic_progress(i / 100.0, 30);

BUILD
    make          build demo, run tests (C99 and C++)
    make win      cross-compile .exe with mingw
    make clean

FILES
    indic.h           the library
    examples/demo.c   usage
    tests/test.c      tests

STATUS
    Windows build compiles; not yet run on a real Windows console.

